KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ __integer_pack yields \{0,1\}: 0\+1==1: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
--
KNOWNBUG: the GCC `__integer_pack(N)` builtin, used by libstdc++'s
std::make_index_sequence (`integer_sequence<T, __integer_pack(N)...>`).  CBMC's
C++ front-end does not know `__integer_pack` ("symbol '__integer_pack' is
unknown"), so the make-sequence alias never produces its element pack and any
deduction/call through it fails ("could not fully type-check 'main'"); the body
is left incomplete and both assertions are skipped -> VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL
vacuously, which does NOT match the desc above (so this registers as a known
bug).  This is the remaining cpp17_apply_basic blocker: std::apply deduces its
__apply_impl index_sequence from a make_index_sequence value; in apply_basic the
__integer_pack error is swallowed during deep decltype/SFINAE resolution and
surfaces as the unresolved `<<type:decltype>>` return type.  __integer_pack is a
GCC-only builtin (Clang uses __make_integer_seq); g++ runs r == 1.  When
supported, __integer_pack generates {0,1}, the body is checked, assertion 2
fails, and the desc matches (promote to CORE).
